00:01:29.582 --> 00:01:31.393
a little bit, because it
was just as I was becoming

00:01:31.393 --> 00:01:33.727
CEO of VaynerMedia, so that might be

00:01:33.727 --> 00:01:34.911
part of the equation as well.

00:01:34.911 --> 00:01:36.246
So that was fun.

00:01:36.246 --> 00:01:39.311
I walked by that this
morning as we set up here.

00:01:39.311 --> 00:01:44.311
Also, India's train is
late, so we have a stand in.

00:01:45.778 --> 00:01:47.914
Danielle, why don't you
tell the Vayner Nation

00:01:47.914 --> 00:01:49.937
who you are and what you do?

00:01:49.937 --> 00:01:52.314
- I'm Danielle, and I'm
the associate director

00:01:52.314 --> 00:01:55.293
of finance here at VaynerMedia.

00:01:55.293 --> 00:01:57.109
Basically I'm in charge of all the finance

00:01:57.109 --> 00:01:59.547
for all of Gary's offices
around the country,

00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:03.007
under the CFO, of course.

00:02:03.007 --> 00:02:04.608
(laughing)

00:02:04.608 --> 00:02:06.060
- Also a big Knicks fan,
which I like about you.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:07.279
- [Danielle] Yes, I'm going tonight.

00:02:07.279 --> 00:02:08.045
- Are you going tonight?

00:02:08.045 --> 00:02:09.416
- [Danielle] Yes, me and my dad go.

00:02:09.416 --> 00:02:13.044
- All right, Danielle, so
let's get into the show!

00:02:13.044 --> 00:02:13.740
You ready?

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:14.495
- Yes.

00:02:14.495 --> 00:02:15.575
- Some big shoes to fill.

00:02:15.575 --> 00:02:16.503
- [Danielle] Yeah, I know.

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:17.595
India has lots of practice.

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:18.605
- Yes.

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:21.255
- [Voiceover] Bernadette
asks: "Hey, Garyvee,

00:02:21.255 --> 00:02:23.272
"I want to relocate: do
I just pick up and move,

00:02:23.272 --> 00:02:25.153
"throw caution to the
wind, or do I wait till

00:02:25.153 --> 00:02:27.503
"I line something up?"

00:02:27.503 --> 00:02:30.610
- Bernadette, I think, the truth is

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.361
I need a little more context
to answer that question.

00:02:33.361 --> 00:02:34.707
Can I see her picture?

00:02:34.707 --> 00:02:35.997
Maybe that can give me a little context.

00:02:35.997 --> 00:02:38.400
Oh, nope, it's some sort
of interesting icon.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.152
Bernadette, I think it comes
down to responsibilities,

00:02:41.152 --> 00:02:42.881
where you are in your life, age group.

00:02:42.881 --> 00:02:45.772
You know, I really do think
that if you're under 30

00:02:45.772 --> 00:02:47.165
everything should be in play.

00:02:47.165 --> 00:02:52.165
You should sleep in the
subways of Singapore,

00:02:52.518 --> 00:02:56.430
you should live on a rock,
you should not eat for a year

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.775
but not die, you know, like everything

00:02:58.775 --> 00:03:02.479
should be in play under 30,
because there is no reason

00:03:02.479 --> 00:03:05.834
to disproportionately deploy
practicality under 30,

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:08.377
because of how long people are gonna live,

00:03:08.377 --> 00:03:12.429
on how much that's an
incredible time in your life,

00:03:12.429 --> 00:03:15.146
and I would, absolutely
if you're under 30,

00:03:15.146 --> 00:03:19.186
just pick up and go, especially
if you're not prima donna.

00:03:19.186 --> 00:03:22.320
See, one of the great advantages
of being an immigrant,

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.294
as I think about the book
that I may want to write

00:03:24.294 --> 00:03:26.454
one day: I Wish Everybody
Was an Immigrant,

00:03:26.454 --> 00:03:30.552
one of the main chapters
is Lack of Entitlement

00:03:30.552 --> 00:03:33.454
or No Prima Donna Gear, right?

00:03:33.454 --> 00:03:35.997
Like, last night my mom and
dad went out to dinner with me

00:03:35.997 --> 00:03:38.656
we were at an event, and we walked

00:03:38.656 --> 00:03:40.629
through the rain with no umbrella,

00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:42.278
and we were just laughing, like

00:03:42.278 --> 00:03:43.903
we just looked at each
other as everybody else

00:03:43.903 --> 00:03:45.517
in this kind of New York
City gala was kind of like

00:03:45.517 --> 00:03:49.256
umbrellas and ubers and we just
kind of looked at each other

00:03:49.256 --> 00:03:51.682
and were like "Belarus,"
you know, it's just

00:03:51.682 --> 00:03:53.913
there's nothing that's kind of sacred.

00:03:53.913 --> 00:03:56.712
Everything's pretty ghetto, meaning that

00:03:56.712 --> 00:04:00.895
if you're able to, Bernadette,

00:04:00.895 --> 00:04:04.185
sleep in a crappy hotel

00:04:04.185 --> 00:04:08.225
or at your friend's couch,
or if you're just not

00:04:08.225 --> 00:04:12.270
in need of cozy things,
then of course you should

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.868
pick up and go, because you could work

00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:17.339
at MacDonalds and live in
a shit hole and be happy,

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:19.220
if that's what's pulling at you.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.786
If you need certain things
to function as a human

00:04:21.786 --> 00:04:26.496
like a clean bathroom or coziness,

00:04:26.496 --> 00:04:29.193
then it becomes harder
for you to pull it off,

00:04:29.193 --> 00:04:31.399
so I think that the graph is really

00:04:31.399 --> 00:04:34.139
completely predecated on
what you need to function.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.995
For me, I need nothing
to function, literally.

00:04:36.995 --> 00:04:39.340
It's so damn scary, and
so everything's in play,

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.860
when it comes to that kind of extreme,

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.497
because I can go to zero.

00:04:43.497 --> 00:04:46.074
I know what it's like to
live on a small budget.

00:04:46.074 --> 00:04:48.106
I don't need fancy things.

00:04:48.106 --> 00:04:50.486
I can wear the same four pieces of clothes

00:04:50.486 --> 00:04:53.702
on rotation for three years,

00:04:53.702 --> 00:04:56.430
so I just think it comes down to you

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:00.424
and your ability to
grind, and the truth is,

00:05:00.424 --> 00:05:02.421
and I've been speaking
to a lot of people over

00:05:02.421 --> 00:05:05.440
this last year, most people
like to say they can grind,

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.447
but they don't, so that's on you.

00:05:08.447 --> 00:05:10.931
- [Voiceover] Louis
asks: "How do you handle

00:05:10.931 --> 00:05:14.053
"price objections when
attempting to close a sale?"

00:05:14.053 --> 00:05:16.899
- I assume price objections
mean that you're asking

00:05:16.899 --> 00:05:21.846
for too much money and they
don't want to pay that?

00:05:21.846 --> 00:05:24.027
What's your take on that, Danielle?

00:05:24.027 --> 00:05:26.210
It's not an easy show, to
just come and get to read

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:27.266
and check out.

00:05:27.266 --> 00:05:28.048
- Are you sure?

00:05:28.048 --> 00:05:29.217
- Yes, I'm very sure.

00:05:29.217 --> 00:05:31.376
- I guess I would say if you
give them a dollar value,

00:05:31.376 --> 00:05:33.873
kind of like we do here when we give

00:05:33.873 --> 00:05:35.591
statements of work to clients
where they approve it,

00:05:35.591 --> 00:05:37.936
they come back with requests to take down

00:05:37.936 --> 00:05:39.469
or gets higher.

00:05:39.469 --> 00:05:42.429
- Do they ever request
to charge them more?

00:05:42.429 --> 00:05:44.217
- Sometimes they ask for more things,

00:05:44.217 --> 00:05:45.448
and then you do change orders,

00:05:45.448 --> 00:05:46.644
and you do get more money that way.

00:05:46.644 --> 00:05:48.814
- Love it!

00:05:48.814 --> 00:05:50.800
Look, I think it's moments in time.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.634
Early on, when I was
building Vayner and I needed

00:05:52.634 --> 00:05:55.537
a leverage of clients and
logos to tell people, yes,

00:05:55.537 --> 00:05:57.452
it's not just I did it for
myself and my family business,

00:05:57.452 --> 00:06:01.632
but for, at the time,
Campbell's, the NHL, Pepsi,

00:06:01.632 --> 00:06:05.069
that mattered, and so I
was willing to take less.

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:06.961
We've talked about spec work ad nausea

00:06:06.961 --> 00:06:08.308
if you watch the show.

00:06:08.308 --> 00:06:09.469
The DRock story.

00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:11.141
So I think it's a leverage game, right?

00:06:11.141 --> 00:06:13.416
Like who has the leverage, and so I think

00:06:13.416 --> 00:06:16.458
that every transaction
has its own cadence.

00:06:16.458 --> 00:06:18.004
There is no blanket statement here.

00:06:18.004 --> 00:06:20.417
You have to understand
what your product is worth,

00:06:20.417 --> 00:06:22.309
but you also have to
think, and this is where

00:06:22.309 --> 00:06:24.852
romance kills people.

00:06:24.852 --> 00:06:28.428
You say that you're worth $150 an hour,

00:06:28.428 --> 00:06:31.040
and you don't quantify that
you need this client right now

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.886
because there isn't good deal flow,

00:06:32.886 --> 00:06:35.603
or you want to buy a ring for your girl,

00:06:35.603 --> 00:06:38.912
or you need to do different things

00:06:38.912 --> 00:06:41.268
besides just shoot weddings
because you want to show

00:06:41.268 --> 00:06:43.509
a better portfolio to get other business.

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:46.388
People are not using other
variables and they go

00:06:46.388 --> 00:06:48.258
well I'm worth $1.50!

00:06:48.258 --> 00:06:49.621
Fuck you!

00:06:49.621 --> 00:06:51.189
You're worth $1.50 in your head,

00:06:51.189 --> 00:06:52.617
the market decides what you're worth.

00:06:52.617 --> 00:06:55.345
You're worth $1.50 if
people will pay you $1.50,

00:06:55.345 --> 00:06:57.957
consistently, always, always and forever.

00:06:57.957 --> 00:07:00.941
You're not worth that, look,
there was two years ago

00:07:00.941 --> 00:07:03.731
where I prematurely tried to
raise my speaking fee higher,

00:07:03.731 --> 00:07:05.469
and the market was like that's great Gary,

00:07:05.469 --> 00:07:07.051
and you're the best speaker ever,

00:07:07.051 --> 00:07:08.525
and this and that, but that is just not

00:07:08.525 --> 00:07:12.113
where your price is at,
and so you're not entitled

00:07:12.113 --> 00:07:14.748
to anything other than
what the people that are

00:07:14.748 --> 00:07:16.584
buying your stuff agree to.

00:07:16.584 --> 00:07:18.916
What you need to be smart
about is understanding

00:07:18.916 --> 00:07:21.412
when's the right time to negotiate down

00:07:21.412 --> 00:07:23.260
because it's in your best
interests, or when are you

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:25.929
negotiating down for no reason at all

00:07:25.929 --> 00:07:27.450
and you're declining your value.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.145
That's on you. That's
being a good salesperson.

00:07:29.145 --> 00:07:30.863
That's being a good operator.

00:07:30.863 --> 00:07:32.500
So, I think that everybody
here needs to have

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:33.835
a balance of both.

00:07:33.835 --> 00:07:35.356
You have to pull from opposite directions.

00:07:35.356 --> 00:07:36.749
When is it in your vested interests?

00:07:36.749 --> 00:07:41.184
And then you deploy humilty
Kool Aid at scale, right?

00:07:41.184 --> 00:07:43.227
The amount of times I will deploy humility

00:07:43.227 --> 00:07:46.717
in a world where my ego
is on fire is off the,

00:07:46.717 --> 00:07:48.429
you know what, Staphon, I want fire here.

00:07:48.429 --> 00:07:50.135
Ego fire. Give me ego fire.

00:07:50.135 --> 00:07:52.098
I've got nothing but ego and bravado,

00:07:52.098 --> 00:07:53.978
but there's plenty of
times I deploy humility

00:07:53.978 --> 00:07:56.892
'cause that's what that
moment's game needs

00:07:56.892 --> 00:07:59.122
to be successful, and so I would tell you

00:07:59.122 --> 00:08:01.943
to not deploy romance.

00:08:01.943 --> 00:08:03.660
This is this and that.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.028
Deploy practicality of the moment.

00:08:09.348 --> 00:08:10.859
Did you like that one?

00:08:10.859 --> 00:08:11.765
- [Danielle] I did like that one.

00:08:11.765 --> 00:08:12.972
- Yeah, it felt like you
gave me a quick little look.

00:08:12.972 --> 00:08:14.424
- [Danielle] And I like the fire going.

00:08:14.424 --> 00:08:15.875
- Wait till you see the
way Staphon edits it.

00:08:15.875 --> 00:08:17.296
Are you gonna watch it?
- Of course I--

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:18.806
- This is an interesting
question, and don't lie--

00:08:18.806 --> 00:08:20.152
- Do you know my dad watches your show?

00:08:20.152 --> 00:08:20.954
- He does?

00:08:20.954 --> 00:08:23.612
- So, if you and A.J.
go to the Dallas game,

00:08:23.612 --> 00:08:25.389
'cause I got my dad tickets for Christmas,

00:08:25.389 --> 00:08:26.886
I want him to meet up with you guys.

00:08:26.886 --> 00:08:28.329
He thinks that you're so awesome.

00:08:28.329 --> 00:08:29.452
- Well, what's your dad's name?

00:08:29.452 --> 00:08:30.497
- Tony.

00:08:31.987 --> 00:08:33.178
- Hey, Tony.

00:08:33.178 --> 00:08:35.547
Two things: one, thank you
for thinking that I'm awesome.

00:08:35.547 --> 00:08:37.393
I think you're awesome,
because of number two,

00:08:37.393 --> 00:08:38.601
I adore your daughter.

00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:40.736
You instilled real work ethic in this one,

00:08:40.736 --> 00:08:42.327
and that, I respect.

00:08:42.327 --> 00:08:43.663
- Thank you.

00:08:43.663 --> 00:08:44.731
- You're welcome. Go ahead.

00:08:44.731 --> 00:08:46.542
- [Voiceover] Nick asks: "It's lonely

00:08:46.542 --> 00:08:48.086
"as an entrepreneur sometimes.

00:08:48.086 --> 00:08:50.862
"How do you cope with that feeling?"

00:08:50.862 --> 00:08:52.778
- You know, for me, I'm built for it.

00:08:52.778 --> 00:08:53.775
I wanna be lonely.

00:08:53.775 --> 00:08:58.094
I want to struggle and grind
and have all the pressure.

00:08:58.094 --> 00:09:01.079
I'm gonna take the last shot in the game

00:09:01.079 --> 00:09:03.028
always, every time.

00:09:03.028 --> 00:09:06.550
It makes me simpatico with

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.880
Staphon's idol Kobe,

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.084
the black mamba who's on this amazing,

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:13.618
it was funny, I was
working out this morning

00:09:13.618 --> 00:09:16.066
and I had to do some cardio
stuff that was hard for me,

00:09:16.066 --> 00:09:18.527
so I'm like Mike, put on TV
so I can watch Sports Center

00:09:18.527 --> 00:09:20.466
and not think about what
we're actually doing,

00:09:20.466 --> 00:09:23.103
and I caught the clip of
the way Kobe last night

00:09:23.103 --> 00:09:24.936
in Philadelphia, and I said to Mike,

00:09:24.936 --> 00:09:26.320
I said, you know what's
so awesome about sports?

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:27.966
It's that if you time it
right, and you know it,

00:09:27.966 --> 00:09:29.777
you can have this kind of farewell tour,

00:09:29.777 --> 00:09:33.237
so I've been thinking about
my farewell entrepreneur tour.

00:09:33.237 --> 00:09:34.398
I don't know how to do that.

00:09:34.398 --> 00:09:37.185
I'm gonna be like 89, 97, be like eeeh,

00:09:37.185 --> 00:09:41.271
but you know, I don't even
remember the question.

00:09:41.271 --> 00:09:43.071
I just wanted to talk
about Kobe's farewell tour.

00:09:43.071 --> 00:09:43.918
What was it again?

00:09:43.918 --> 00:09:45.195
Oh! Being lonely.

00:09:45.195 --> 00:09:47.378
Look, the reason I brought up Kobe is,

00:09:47.378 --> 00:09:48.795
Kobe wants to take the last shot.

00:09:48.795 --> 00:09:50.060
Winners wanna take the last shot.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:51.906
You want to take the high with the low.

00:09:51.906 --> 00:09:56.004
When you are truly an A, and
actual pure-bred entrepreneur,

00:09:56.004 --> 00:09:58.037
you don't know anything else than

00:09:58.037 --> 00:09:59.604
getting the accolades or getting shit on

00:09:59.604 --> 00:10:00.919
when you don't execute.

00:10:00.919 --> 00:10:02.785
Actually, from first, you
know, it's really interesting.

00:10:02.785 --> 00:10:05.722
I had a 100th of a second,
because I'm concerned

00:10:05.722 --> 00:10:07.463
about macroeconomic climates,

00:10:07.463 --> 00:10:08.808
for a 100th of a second yesterday,

00:10:08.808 --> 00:10:09.992
which is unheard of for me, I was like

00:10:09.992 --> 00:10:12.384
woo, what if Vayner took a step back

00:10:12.384 --> 00:10:14.241
and I had to deal with
people being like, oh,

00:10:14.241 --> 00:10:15.936
you're not running this business well,

00:10:15.936 --> 00:10:17.353
or what's going on?

00:10:17.353 --> 00:10:18.061
It's so funny.

00:10:18.061 --> 00:10:19.385
I thought of it for a 100th of a second,

00:10:19.385 --> 00:10:20.882
and then I got so happy.

00:10:20.882 --> 00:10:23.204
I got so happy because
I quickly thought about

00:10:23.204 --> 00:10:25.735
the second chess move, which
was, for whatever reason,

00:10:25.735 --> 00:10:27.943
couple of our clients,
as you know, are starting

00:10:27.943 --> 00:10:29.961
to become very big clients
and I don't like them

00:10:29.961 --> 00:10:31.610
being too much a percentage of my business

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.864
'cause they can go away the next day.

00:10:32.864 --> 00:10:34.385
I don't like that, so that maybe is why

00:10:34.385 --> 00:10:35.665
